\section{Two GDAL defaults worth knowing}{

\code{algorithm = "linear"} on its own does not stop at the convex hull. GDAL's
default is \code{radius=-1}, an infinite search, so a cell in no triangle takes
the value of the nearest point instead of being left out. That is
extrapolation, it is silent, and it is why \code{"linear:radius=0.0"} rather than
\code{"linear"} is the one that matches \code{\link[=grid_barycentric]{grid_barycentric()}}.

Second, \code{gdal_grid} fills cells it could not estimate with 0 and does not
record that 0 as the band's no data value, so nothing downstream can tell it
apart from a real measurement -- and 0 is a real temperature, a real
elevation and a real anomaly. This function therefore appends \code{nodata=nan}
unless the algorithm string already sets one. That both fills the empty
cells with \code{NaN} and tags the band, and \pkg{gdalraster} turns the tagged
value into \code{NA} on the way back, so an explicit \code{nodata=-9999} arrives here
as \code{NA} just the same.
}

\section{Why sf}{

\code{gdal_grid} is reachable from R only through \code{\link[sf:gdal_utils]{sf::gdal_utils()}}.
\pkg{gdalraster} wraps \code{warp}, \code{translate} and \code{rasterize} but not
\code{GDALGrid}, and the unified \code{gdal} command line added in GDAL 3.11 has no
grid subcommand either, so there is nothing for it to wrap yet.
}

